# SIH 2026 Software Problems — Full Domain Analysis and Plain-Language Guide

**Source:** User-supplied workbook `SIH_2026_Software_Problems_Grouped(1).xlsx`. The report covers all rows in its `Software_Grouped` sheet.

> **Important:** “Easy,” “Moderate,” “Advanced,” and “Very high” are execution estimates for a capable student software/AI team. They are not official SIH scores. The simplified wording preserves the intent of the official titles but should be checked against the original statement before submission.

## Executive recommendation

Do not choose only because a statement contains “AI.” Choose a problem where the team can demonstrate a complete user workflow, has access to the needed data, can create a fallback when an API fails, and can measure success. For a normal 4–5 person team, the strongest choices are workflow-heavy software in Clean & Green, Smart Automation, Transportation & Logistics, Heritage & Culture, Travel & Tourism, selected Education, Agriculture, and selected Miscellaneous statements.

A problem is marked **Eliminate for now** when a convincing solution would require research-grade models, specialized scientific data, defense/security expertise, clinical validation, hardware/field trials, autonomous control, cryptography, quantum computing, or operational safety guarantees. That does not mean the problem is impossible; it means it is a poor default choice without specialist capability.

## 1. Decision categories

| Decision | Meaning | What to build if selected |
|---|---|---|
| Shortlist / workable | Best candidates for a complete prototype | End-to-end product, one bounded AI feature, seeded/public data, measurable demo. |
| Keep with domain caveat | Workable workflow, sensitive claims | Decision-support or management tool; show limitations and expert review. |
| Specialist / bounded prototype | Possible with specialist team | Simulator, offline analyzer, dashboard, or research proof of concept. |
| Eliminate for now | High delivery and credibility risk | Do not select unless your team already has the required expertise, data, and infrastructure. |

## 2. Overall distribution

The workbook contains **172 software problem statements** across **18 domains**.

| Difficulty | Count | Decision implication |
|---|---:|---|
| Easy | 91 | Usually workable |
| Moderate | 17 | Usually workable |
| Advanced | 24 | Needs specialist scope |
| Very high | 40 | Eliminate by default |

